,"la":41.023235,"lo":28.975455,"d":903},{"n":"QNB","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":41.023016,"lo":28.975474,"d":913},{"n":"Ziraat Bankası","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":41.031884,"lo":28.976226,"d":914},{"n":"Türkiye İş Bankası","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":41.031571,"lo":28.97587,"d":919},{"n":"PTT Beyoğlu","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":41.03302,"lo":28.977359,"d":926,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:30-17:00; Sa-Su off","w":"https://ptt.gov.tr/","ph":"+90 212 252 01 40"},{"n":"Vakıfbank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":41.02366,"lo":28.974855,"d":928},{"n":"Tüm","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":41.026001,"lo":28.973939,"d":934},{"n":"Akbank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":41.034138,"lo":28.979091,"d":935},{"n":"Halkbank","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":41.035496,"lo":28.984834,"d":944},{"n":"Vakıfbank","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":41.028269,"lo":28.973851,"d":945},{"n":"Nusretiye Clock Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.026711,"lo":28.982875,"d":181,"ds":"Nusretiye Clock Tower, aka Tophane Clock Tower, is a clock tower situated at Tophane, a neighborhood in Beyoğlu district of Istanbul, Turkey, next to Nusretiye Mosque and Tophane Kiosk at the European waterfront of Bosphorus. It was ordered by the Ottoman sultan Abdulmejid I (1823–1861), designed by architect Garabet Amira Balyan and completed in 1848.","wpu":"Nusretiye Clock Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Istanbul%27s_oldest_clocktower_has_found_new_life_as_the_centre_of_a_square_behind_Galataport_with_the_Nusretiye_Mosque_behind_it.jpg/960px-Istanbul%27s_oldest_clocktower_has_found_new_life_as_the_centre_of_a_square_behind_Galataport_with_the_Nusretiye_Mosque_behind_it.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kılıç Ali Pasha Complex","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.026373,"lo":28.980928,"d":348,"ds":"The Kılıç Ali Pasha Mosque (Turkish: Kılıç Ali Paşa Cami) is a mosque at the heart of a complex designed and built between 1580 and 1587 by Mimar Sinan, who at the time was in his 90s. The mosque itself was constructed in 1578–1580. The complex is located in the Tophane neighbourhood of the Beyoğlu district of Istanbul, Turkey.","wpu":"Kılıç Ali Pasha Complex","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/K%C4%B1l%C4%B1%C3%A7_Ali_Pasha_Complex.jpg/960px-K%C4%B1l%C4%B1%C3%A7_Ali_Pasha_Complex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Galataport","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.024833,"lo":28.98101,"d":412,"ds":"Galataport is a 400,000-square-metre (4,300,000 sq ft) mixed-use development located along 1.2 km (0.75 mi) of shore in the Karaköy (formerly called Galata) neighbourhood of Istanbul, Turkey on the European shore of the Bosporus strait near its confluence with the Golden Horn right in the heart of the city.","wpu":"Galataport","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Overview_of_Galataport_from_Cihangir_showing_Istanbul_Painting_and_Sculpture_Museum_on_left_and_Nusretiye_Mosque_on_right_with_Asian_shore_and_Princes%27_Islands_in_the_background.jpg/960px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Museum of Innocence (museum)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.030906,"lo":28.979788,"d":616,"ds":"The Museum of Innocence (Turkish: Masumiyet Müzesi) is a museum in a 19th-century house in Istanbul (Çukurcuma) created by novelist Orhan Pamuk as a companion to his novel The Museum of Innocence. The museum and the novel were created in tandem, centred on the stories of two Istanbul families. On 17 May 2014, the museum was announced as the winner of the 2014 European Museum of the Year Award.","wpu":"The Museum of Innocence (museum)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/The_Museum_of_Innocence.jpg/960px-The_Museum_of_Innocence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Crimea Memorial Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.0277,"lo":28.9771,"d":667,"ds":"The Crimea Memorial Church, officially known as Christ Church, Galata, is a Church of England church in the Beyoglu - Taksim district of Istanbul, Turkey.","wpu":"Crimea Memorial Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Crimea_Memorial_Church.jpg/960px-Crimea_Memorial_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Molla Çelebi Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.0321,"lo":28.9906,"d":736,"ds":"The Molla Çelebi Mosque (Turkish: Molla Çelebi Camii), sometimes known as the Fındıklı Mosque (Turkish: Fındıklı Camii) is a 16th-century Ottoman mosque located in the Fındıklı neighbourhood of the Beyoğlu district of Istanbul, Turkey. It was commissioned by Kazasker Mehmet Vusuli Efendi, chief judge of Istanbul and designed by the imperial architect Mimar Sinan.","wpu":"Molla Çelebi Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Findikli_Molla_Celebi_Camii_-_January_2011_-_01.jpg/960px-Findikli_Molla_Celebi_Camii_-_January_2011_-_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ashkenazi Synagogue of Istanbul","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.02521,"lo":28.97509,"d":854,"ds":"The Ashkenazi Synagogue (Turkish: Aşkenazi Sinagogu) is an Orthodox Jewish congregation and synagogue, located near the Galata Tower in Karaköy neighborhood of Beyoğlu in Istanbul, Turkey. The congregation worships in the Ashkenazi rite. It is the only currently active Ashkenazi synagogue in Istanbul open to visits and prayers.","wpu":"Ashkenazi Synagogue of Istanbul","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Istanbul_Ashkenazi_Sinagogue_Interior.JPG/960px-Istanbul_Ashkenazi_Sinagogue_Interior.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yeraltı Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.022463,"lo":28.976702,"d":859,"ds":"The Yeraltı Mosque (Turkish: Yeraltı Camii, lit. 'Underground Mosque') is a mosque located in the Karaköy part of the Beyoğlu district in Istanbul, Turkey. The mosque's name is derived from the fact that its prayer hall and main features of it are all underground. It is sometimes known as Kurşunlu Mahzen, as it was a place where the belongings of captured Umayyad warriors were sealed away with molten lead.","wpu":"Yeraltı Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/20110722_south_entrance_Yeralti_Cami_Karakoy_Istanbul_Turkey.jpg/960px-20110722_south_entrance_Yeralti_Cami_Karakoy_Istanbul_Turkey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Atlas Cinema","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.0339,"lo":28.9796,"d":890,"ds":"The Atlas Cinema also known as Istanbul Cinema Museum (Turkish: Atlas Sineması) is a movie theatre situated at İstiklal Avenue in the Beyoğlu district of Istanbul, Turkey. It opened in 1948. Between 2019 and 2021, it underwent a restoration.","wpu":"Atlas Cinema","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Atlas_Sinemas%C4%B1%2C_%C4%B0stanbul_Sinema_M%C3%BCzesi.jpg/960px-Atlas_Sinemas%C4%B1%2C_%C4%B0stanbul_Sinema_M%C3%BCzesi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Taksim Surp Harutyun Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.035057,"lo":28.984126,"d":898,"ds":"Taksim Surp Harutyun Church (Armenian: Սուրբ Յարութիւն Եկեղեցի), is an Armenian Church in the Beyoğlu district of Istanbul. The original date of first construction is not exactly known but the church was removed in 1846, closed down in 1890 and rebuilt in 1895 by the architects Hovhannes and Mıgırdiç Esayan. The number of the Christian church visitors fell after the Armenian Genocide of 1915.","wpu":"Taksim Surp Harutyun Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Taksim_Surp_Harutyun_Church.jpg/960px-Taksim_Surp_Harutyun_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Karaköy Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.0217,"lo":28.9767,"d":912,"ds":"Karaköy Pier (Turkish: Karaköy iskelesi) is a ferry landing on the Golden Horn in Beyoğlu, Istanbul. Located along Rıhtım Avenue, just east of the Galata Bridge, Şehir Hatları operates many ferries from Karaköy to Kadıköy, Bostancı, Üsküdar and to piers along the Golden Horn, as far west as Eyüp. Karaköy pier first opened in 1959, replacing an older pier on the Galata Bridge, and has been rebuilt three times since.","wpu":"Karaköy Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Karak%C3%B6y_Mars_2013_02.jpg/960px-Karak%C3%B6y_Mars_2013_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of Saint Anthony of Padua, Istanbul","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.0325,"lo":28.9764,"d":945,"ds":"The Church of St. Anthony of Padua (Turkish: Sent Antuan Kilisesi), alternatively known as Sant'Antonio di Padova Church or S. Antonio di Padova, is the largest Catholic church in Istanbul, Turkey. It is located on İstiklal Avenue in the Beyoğlu district.","wpu":"Church of Saint Anthony of Padua, Istanbul","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Istanbul_asv2021-11_img71_StAnthony_of_Padua_Church.jpg/960px-Istanbul_asv2021-11_img71_StAnthony_of_Padua_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Changa (restaurant)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.035833,"lo":28.984806,"d":982,"ds":"Changa is a restaurant in Istanbul, Turkey, established in 1999 and located close to Taksim Square. It is owned by restaurateurs Tarık Bayazıt and Savaş Ertunç, and operated under the consultancy and supervision of the renowned Kiwi chef Peter Gordon. In 2002, Changa was chosen 39th of the world's 50 top restaurants by the Restaurant magazine.","wpu":"Changa (restaurant)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Changa%2C_on_S%C4%B1raselviler_street_%284580770651%29.jpg/960px-Changa%2C_on_S%C4%B1raselviler_street_%284580770651%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Istanbul is the largest city in Turkey, a megacity, constituting the country's economic, cultural, and historical center. With a population of over 15 million, it is home to 18% of the population of Turkey. Istanbul is among the largest cities in Europe and in the world by population.","wpu":"Istanbul","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Historical_peninsula_and_modern_skyline_of_Istanbul.jpg/960px-Historical_peninsula_and_modern_skyline_of_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}